    "poster_id": 171117,
    "post_rendered": "The point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > lies inside, or on the boundary of, the triangle <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" >.</span> Denote by <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/c/8fc4dc9077efc341b5311ffcd9db3d1e335f586a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d_{a}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" >,</span> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/8/f086ad2a2639cfbf37816de8a1dd62c05ef76bef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d_{b}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/9/9699b8e340ca38f62a9cfa158b4a73e7a58f16af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d_{c}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" > the distances between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > and <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"12\" >,</span> <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/5/e657259c0995d2c36d46744bb4aa81471e6575ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CA$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"13\" >,</span> and <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\"  width=\"27\" height=\"13\" >,</span> respectively. Prove that <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/2/9c266060bf6ac8f04c62a7bd94719017a84d41cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\max\\{AP,BP,CP \\} \\ge \\sqrt{d_{a}^{2}+d_{b}^{2}+d_{c}^{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"287\" height=\"32\" >.</span> When does the equality holds?",
    "post_canonical": "The point $P$ lies inside, or on the boundary of, the triangle $ABC$. Denote by $d_{a}$, $d_{b}$ and $d_{c}$ the distances between $P$ and $BC$, $CA$, and $AB$, respectively. Prove that $\\max\\{AP,BP,CP \\} \\ge \\sqrt{d_{a}^{2}+d_{b}^{2}+d_{c}^{2}}$. When does the equality holds?",
